

PASSWD(1)           UNIX Programmer's Manual           PASSWD(1)



NAME
     passwd - change a login password

SYNOPSIS
     passwd [ user ]

DESCRIPTION
     Passwd changes a password in /etc/passwd.  With no
     argument it changes the password of the account running it;
     with a user argument it changes that account's.

     The new password is asked for twice, with echo off:

          New password:
          Retype new password:

     and nothing is written until the two agree.  An empty
     answer to either prompt -- including the empty string an
     end-of-file produces -- prints

          Password unchanged.

     and exits without touching the file, so a control-D is
     always a safe way out.  SIGHUP, SIGINT, SIGQUIT and SIGTSTP
     are ignored for the whole run, so control-D is also the only
     way out: a signal arriving between the two renames below
     would leave /etc/passwd missing rather than merely stale.

     A user who is not root must supply the current password
     before being asked for a new one, and may only name their
     own account.  An account whose password field is EMPTY is
     locked -- login(1) fails closed on it -- so a non-root user
     cannot change one: there is no current password to prove,
     and treating that as "nothing to prove" would let the owner
     of a locked account unlock it.  Root is asked for neither.
     Every FGX process
     currently runs as uid 0 -- the kernel answers getuid(2) from
     a single global -- so nothing reaches that arm yet; it is
     written for the day credentials become per-process rather
     than deleted and reinvented then.

     A non-root password must be at least six characters and must
     not be entirely lower case.  Root is exempt: root must be
     able to set a password the rules forbid, to recover an
     account or to bring up a machine that is on no network at
     all, and can edit /etc/passwd directly in any case.

HOW THE FILE IS REPLACED
     The live /etc/passwd is never opened for writing.  The new
     database is built in /etc/ptmp -- claimed with O_EXCL, so a
     second passwd already in the middle of a rewrite is told the
     password file is busy -- and only then swapped in:

          unlink /etc/opasswd
          rename /etc/passwd -> /etc/opasswd
          rename /etc/ptmp   -> /etc/passwd

     Two renames, not one, because FGX's rename(2) renames in
     place only when the target does NOT exist; renaming over a
     live /etc/passwd returns EXDEV (see mv(1)).  The cost is a
     two-syscall window in which /etc/passwd is absent and a
     login started inside it fails.  The benefit is that no
     failure can leave a truncated or half-written passwd file,
     which is the one outcome nobody can log in to repair, and
     that /etc/opasswd holds the previous database afterwards.
     If the second rename fails the first is undone.

     Lines other than the one being changed are copied through
     byte for byte, including the file's leading comment: only
     the second colon-separated field of the matching line is
     replaced.  If the entry is not found, or a line is longer
     than 255 characters, nothing is renamed and /etc/passwd is
     left exactly as it was.

SECURITY
     THE THREAT MODEL IS DELIBERATELY LIMITED.  The stored hash
     is not DES.  It is the tagged form

          $f$xy$hhhhhhhh

     where xy is a two-character salt and hhhhhhhh is eight hex
     digits -- see crypt(3).  It stops a shoulder-surfer and a
     casual reader of /etc/passwd.  It is not a defence against
     an attacker who has the file and wants the password.
     Choosing a longer password does not change that: the hash is
     eight hex digits wide whatever you type.

     The salt is taken from the clock, not fixed, so two accounts
     that choose the same password do not get the same hash and
     /etc/passwd does not advertise the fact.

     /etc/opasswd is left behind holding the PREVIOUS database,
     including the previous hash for the account just changed.
     It is not read by anything; it exists so a failed swap is
     recoverable.  Remove it if that bothers you.

FILES
     /etc/passwd           user database
     /etc/ptmp             the new database, while it is written
     /etc/opasswd          the previous database

SEE ALSO
     login(1), crypt(3), passwd(5)

DIAGNOSTICS
     passwd: password file busy -- try again later.
          /etc/ptmp exists, so another passwd is partway through
          a rewrite.  If none is, it was interrupted; the live
          file is intact and removing /etc/ptmp clears this.

     passwd: /etc/passwd: no entry for name
          The account was in the database when it was looked up
          and not when it was copied.  Nothing is renamed.

     passwd: /etc/passwd is MISSING -- recover with: mv
     /etc/opasswd /etc/passwd
          The swap failed partway AND the rollback failed too, so
          the database is on disk only under its backup name.
          Nothing else on the machine can log a user in until
          that mv is run.  Do it from the shell that printed
          this, before logging out.

BUGS
     There is no password ageing, no login class, and no way to
     lock an account other than emptying its password field --
     which login(1) treats as "cannot log in", not as "no
     password required".  Neither vipw(8) nor chpass(1) exists,
     so /etc/ptmp is contended for by passwd alone.



